// Gin Rummy — stress-test DSL encoding (NOT part of the canonical corpus).
//
// Source of rules: https://www.pagat.com/rummy/ginrummy.html ("Gin Rummy",
// fetched live per CLAUDE.md). Two players, standard 52-card deck, ten cards
// each. Each turn: draw from stock or take the top discard (if you took the
// discard, you must discard a different card), then discard. A player may
// knock once their deadwood (point value of cards not part of a valid
// combination) is 10 or less; going out with zero deadwood is gin. Melds are
// a set (3-4 of the same rank) or a run (3+ consecutive same-suit cards, ace
// low). Score: gin = 20 + opponent's deadwood; ordinary knock, if the
// knocker's deadwood is strictly lower, = the difference; undercut (knocker's
// deadwood >= opponent's) = the difference + 10, credited to the opponent.
// Game to 100.
//
// THE STRESS POINT this file is built to probe: minimal-deadwood meld
// detection — partitioning a hand into melds that minimizes leftover point
// value. See the "GAP" comments below and the accompanying report for the
// full finding; the short version is: the DSL's expression sublanguage can
// only aggregate flatly over a *single* zone's contents (`sum`/`count`/
// `max`/`min over <zone> as x: …`) or quantify over `player`/`team` — there
// is no subset enumeration and named `function`s are non-recursive, so
// "compute the partition of this hand that minimizes deadwood" cannot be
// written as an expression. What the DSL *can* do is VALIDATE a declared
// partition (a small, fixed-size group's "same rank" / "same suit and
// consecutive" test reduces to a `max == min` / `max - min == count - 1`
// aggregate, which the language handles cleanly). So melding here is modeled
// as an explicit incremental player decision — cards move one at a time into
// one of three meld-in-progress zones, each addition gated by an "does this
// legally extend the current partial group" predicate — rather than an
// engine-computed optimum. Under uniform-random play this means a hand
// rarely reaches a completed meld (the chooser must, by chance, keep
// extending the same group instead of picking `finish_arranging`) — that is
// exploring a combinatorial decision space randomly (same phenomenon as
// Bridge rarely bidding a slam under random play), not a defect.
//
// Declared simplifications (every one deliberate, not a silent drop):
//   - No layoff: after a knock, the non-knocking opponent does not get to
//     extend cards onto the knocker's exposed melds. The task brief
//     explicitly allows skipping this ("skippable — record"). The opponent's
//     deadwood is their own best *self-declared* partition of their own
//     hand (they still run the same meld-building mechanic on their own
//     hand once the knock happens), just without borrowing space in the
//     knocker's melds. This makes undercuts somewhat likelier than the real
//     game under random play, since laying off would only ever lower a
//     defender's deadwood.
//   - Card point values (A=1, 2-10=pip, J/Q/K=10) are derived from the
//     stdlib `rank_value()` function (itself derived from this file's own
//     `ranking:` declaration: `ranking: K Q J … A` makes ace rank_value 0),
//     NOT from the stdlib `card_value()` function. `card_value()` reads a
//     per-deck point table that is empty for `standard52` in the current
//     runtime (`cardlang/runtime/values.py`: `"standard52": Deck(..., values={})`),
//     and the DSL's `cards:` header has no syntax for a game to supply its
//     own point table (only a bare deck name — the "compose with extras"
//     form library.md sketches isn't implemented in the grammar). Cribbage
//     solved the analogous problem with a bespoke Python runtime primitive
//     (`peg_value`); since writing Python is forbidden here, `rank_value()`
//     is repurposed instead — a workaround, not a Python addition.
//   - Dropped: the box bonus (+20 per hand won) and the match-end 100/200
//     "additional bonus" Pagat describes. Pure decorative arithmetic on top
//     of the ordinary hand score; doesn't exercise any language capability
//     this stress test cares about, so it was cut for scope, not silently
//     lost — noted here.
//   - Dropped: the traditional dealer/non-dealer first-turn refusal-of-the-
//     upcard wrinkle some rule sets use. The non-dealer simply takes an
//     ordinary first turn.
//   - Simplified: knocking does not separately model laying a final card
//     face down. The knock directly computes deadwood from all 11 held
//     cards (melded groups + hand remainder) at the moment of the knock
//     decision, and there is no separate "big gin" bonus for melding all 11
//     without a leftover discard (Pagat's page, as fetched, does not mention
//     one) — an all-11-melded hand scores as an ordinary gin.
//   - Dealer alternates every hand (as Cribbage does in this corpus) rather
//     than "loser deals next" or similar variants some rule sets use.
//
// One thing that is NOT a simplification, worth flagging as a positive
// result: "if you took the top discard, you must discard a different card"
// IS enforced, structurally rather than by comparing card identities (the
// language has no way to bind "the specific card a movement just moved" to
// a value for later comparison — see the GAP comment on `newly_drawn`
// below). A drawn discard-pile card is held in a separate private zone and
// only merged into the hand *after* an ordinary discard (or immediately, if
// attempting a knock, or immediately for a stock draw) — so the just-taken
// card is simply not a member of the pool `discard` selects from when the
// restriction should apply.

game GinRummy {

  players: 2
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52
  // Ace low, as the real game requires (A-2-3 is a run; A-K-Q is not).
  // Listed highest-to-lowest per this project's `ranking:` convention (the
  // first-listed rank gets the highest `rank_value`; see driver.py).
  ranking: K Q J 10 9 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 A

  zones {
    deck                 : Deck
    hand[player]         : Hand<player>
    // GAP: holds a just-drawn card before it's decided whether the "must
    // discard a different card" restriction applies to it (see header). Also
    // doubles as the only way to give arranging access to a discard-pile
    // pickup: `attempt_knock` merges it into `hand` before arranging runs.
    newly_drawn[player]  : Hand<player>
    // The single pick-up-able card (0 or 1 cards) vs. everything discarded
    // before it (still public, no longer drawable) — split so "the top of
    // the discard pile" is a zone-emptiness check, not a positional query
    // (the `Discard` library type carries no declared card order).
    discard_top          : Discard
    discard_history      : Discard
    // Meld-in-progress zones: private (owner only) while a player is still
    // deciding groupings. Three slots is exactly enough for a post-draw
    // 11-card hand (3+4+4 = 11; 3+3+4 = 10).
    stageA[player]       : Hand<player>
    stageB[player]       : Hand<player>
    stageC[player]       : Hand<player>
    // Revealed melds and revealed leftover cards, populated only once a
    // knock actually happens — the movement into these public zones IS the
    // reveal (derived from zone visibility, not a hand-authored "reveal"
    // step; see decisions.md "Knowledge, visibility, and the projection
    // model"). Both the knocker and the opponent commit here so the
    // observation trail matches what a real player would see at showdown.
    meldA[player]        : PlayerPile<player>
    meldB[player]        : PlayerPile<player>
    meldC[player]        : PlayerPile<player>
    shown_deadwood[player] : PlayerPile<player>
  }

  state {
    // Game-level: persists across hands.
    score[player]   : Integer = 0
    // A hard, luck-independent backstop on top of the score threshold below —
    // see the tractability note there. Bounds total simulated time regardless
    // of how many hands a run of bad luck needs.
    hands_played    : Integer = 0
  }

  // GAP/simplification: Pagat plays to 100. A `round offering […] until …`
  // decision node is re-evaluated (all four vocabulary guards, each a
  // several-deep nested-function aggregate) at every one of up to ~11 steps
  // of every arranging attempt, and — because there is no combinatorial
  // search — a hand only reaches deadwood <= 10 when uniform-random choice
  // happens to grow two or three *compatible* groups at once, which is rare
  // (empirically well under 1% of hands even with the forced-continuation
  // trick below). A full race to 100 needs enough successful hands to add up
  // that it does not reliably finish inside this harness's per-seed timeout
  // — a consequence of the expressiveness gap (no partition search means no
  // cheap way to know a hand is promising), not a rules simplification in
  // the ordinary sense. The threshold is cut to keep 20 playouts tractable;
  // every other rule (deadwood math, gin/knock/undercut amounts, meld
  // validity) is unchanged. `hands_played >= 180` is a second, luck-independent
  // backstop: rare-success variance means a threshold alone could still make
  // one unlucky seed in twenty run long, so the game also just stops after
  // 180 hands (`winner: highest score` then resolves whatever the tally is,
  // including a 0-0 tie if no seat ever knocked) — bounding total simulated
  // work (empirically ~40-45s at the cap, comfortably under this harness's
  // 60s-per-seed budget) regardless of how the dice fall. Confirmed via
  // `stress-test/run_playout.py … 20`: 20/20 complete, no seed times out.
  phase hand_sequence repeats until (any player p: score[p] >= 25) or hands_played >= 180 {
    state {
      dealer          : Player  = 0
      active          : Player  = 0
      knocked         : Boolean = false
      stock_exhausted : Boolean = false
      knocker         : Player? = none
      knock_deadwood  : Integer = 0
      opp_deadwood    : Integer = 0
      drew            : Boolean = false
      acted           : Boolean = false
      attempting      : Boolean = false
      arranging_done  : Boolean = false
      // GAP/simplification: taking the discard pile never touches the stock,
      // so a uniform-random policy that keeps picking `draw_discard` (a
      // legal choice every single turn) can run indefinitely without ever
      // reaching Pagat's "stock down to two" cutoff — a real but vanishingly
      // rare possibility in skilled play, pathological under random
      // exploration. Bounding turns and cancelling the hand the same way
      // stock exhaustion does keeps this a faithful-enough approximation
      // while keeping playouts finite.
      turns           : Integer = 0
    }

    before_each {
      move all cards to deck
      shuffle deck
      deal 10 cards from deck to each hand
      move one card from deck to discard_top   // the 21st card, turned face up
      dealer := the player where player != dealer
      active := the player where player != dealer   // non-dealer plays first
      knocked := false
      stock_exhausted := false
      knocker := none
      knock_deadwood := 0
      opp_deadwood := 0
      turns := 0
      hands_played += 1
    }

    phase play {
      // No `active_rules`/`legal_moves` here: every decision runs on the
      // `round offering […] from … over … until …` form (a single-participant
      // ring — `over players where player == active` never shrinks, so the
      // ring simply keeps re-offering the same player until their `until`
      // flag flips), matching Bridge's auction phase, which needs neither
      // clause either since its vocabulary lives entirely in `round offering`.
      repeat until knocked or stock_exhausted {
        drew := false
        round offering [draw_stock, draw_discard] from active over players where player == active until drew

        attempting := false
        acted := false
        round offering [discard, attempt_knock] from active over players where player == active until acted

        if attempting {
          arranging_done := false
          round offering [add_to_meld_a, add_to_meld_b, add_to_meld_c, finish_arranging]
                from active over players where player == active until arranging_done

          if meld_deadwood(active) <= 10 {
            knocked := true
            knocker := active
            knock_deadwood := meld_deadwood(active)

            // The reveal: moving into these public (`identity to all`) zones
            // is what makes the knocker's hand known to the opponent —
            // derived from zone visibility, not an explicit "reveal" op
            // (which isn't implemented in this runtime; see the report).
            move all cards from stageA[active] to meldA[active]
            move all cards from stageB[active] to meldB[active]
            move all cards from stageC[active] to meldC[active]
            move all cards from hand[active] to shown_deadwood[active]

            // No layoff (see header): the opponent runs the same
            // meld-building mechanic on their own hand, but may not extend
            // it into the knocker's exposed groups.
            let opponent = the player where player != active
            arranging_done := false
            round offering [add_to_meld_a, add_to_meld_b, add_to_meld_c, finish_arranging]
                  from opponent over players where player == opponent until arranging_done
            opp_deadwood := meld_deadwood(opponent)

            move all cards from stageA[opponent] to meldA[opponent]
            move all cards from stageB[opponent] to meldB[opponent]
            move all cards from stageC[opponent] to meldC[opponent]
            move all cards from hand[opponent] to shown_deadwood[opponent]
          } else {
            // The arrangement didn't reach a legal knock: put everything
            // back and discard normally instead. (A consequence of not
            // being able to check "can this player knock" *before* the
            // arranging decision — see the report's info-set note: this
            // path does leak a little more than the real game would, since
            // the attempted groups sat briefly in public zones.)
            move all cards from stageA[active] to hand[active]
            move all cards from stageB[active] to hand[active]
            move all cards from stageC[active] to hand[active]
            // Reuse `discard`'s own effect (via a one-shot single-participant
            // round) rather than repeating its archiving logic inline — and
            // a `chosen` movement directly in phase-body flow has no acting
            // player bound, so it must run through a round/offer form anyway.
            acted := false
            round offering [discard] from active over players where player == active until acted
          }
        }

        if not knocked {
          active := the player where player != active
          turns := turns + 1
          if (count over deck as c: true) <= 2 or turns >= 60 { stock_exhausted := true }   // cancelled, no score
        }
      }
    }

    phase scoring {
      if not stock_exhausted {
        let opponent = the player where player != knocker
        if knock_deadwood == 0 {
          score[knocker] += 20 + opp_deadwood                    // gin
        } else {
          if knock_deadwood < opp_deadwood {
            score[knocker] += opp_deadwood - knock_deadwood         // ordinary knock
          } else {
            score[opponent] += (knock_deadwood - opp_deadwood) + 10 // undercut
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  winner: highest score
}

// === Turn move vocabulary ===

move_type draw_stock {
  when: (count over deck as c: true) > 0
  effect {
    move one card from deck to newly_drawn[actor]
    move all cards from newly_drawn[actor] to hand[actor]   // no restriction on a stock draw
    drew := true
  }
}

move_type draw_discard {
  when: discard_top is not empty
  effect {
    // Held back, NOT merged into hand[actor] yet — see the header note and
    // the `discard`/`attempt_knock` effects below for why.
    move one card from discard_top to newly_drawn[actor]
    drew := true
  }
}

move_type discard {
  effect {
    // Selecting only from hand[actor] (not newly_drawn[actor]) is what
    // enforces "if you took the discard, you must discard a different
    // card" — the just-taken card structurally isn't in this pool yet.
    if discard_top is not empty { move all cards from discard_top to discard_history }
    move chosen 1 card from hand[actor] to discard_top
    move all cards from newly_drawn[actor] to hand[actor]
    acted := true
  }
}

move_type attempt_knock {
  effect {
    // Arranging needs access to a held-back discard pickup too (no
    // restriction applies to *melding* it, only to discarding it back).
    move all cards from newly_drawn[actor] to hand[actor]
    attempting := true
    acted := true
  }
}

// One move type per meld slot (functions can't take a zone as a parameter in
// this language, so the identical logic is written out per slot rather than
// factored — see the report). Each move adds exactly one card, chosen from
// the cards that legally extend the slot's *current* partial group: empty
// (any card seeds it), matching the group's established rank (building a
// set), or matching its established suit and adjacent to its current
// high/low card (building a run). Because every earlier addition passed this
// same check, a slot's contents are a valid partial run or set by
// construction at every intermediate size — `meld_valid_*` below re-derives
// full validity from scratch anyway, both as a belt-and-suspenders check and
// because it is the direct answer to "can the language validate a declared
// partition" (see the report).

move_type add_to_meld_a {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if extends_a(c, actor) then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect { move chosen 1 card from hand[actor] where c => extends_a(c, actor) to stageA[actor] }
}

move_type add_to_meld_b {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if extends_b(c, actor) then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect { move chosen 1 card from hand[actor] where c => extends_b(c, actor) to stageB[actor] }
}

move_type add_to_meld_c {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if extends_c(c, actor) then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect { move chosen 1 card from hand[actor] where c => extends_c(c, actor) to stageC[actor] }
}

// Guarded (not unconditionally offered): whenever an *already-started* slot
// (non-empty, not yet full) still has a card in hand that would extend it,
// finishing is illegal — the arranging round must keep growing that group
// rather than abandoning it. Starting a brand-new empty slot stays a genuine
// (random) choice; only "walk away from a group already in progress that
// could still grow" is forced closed. Without this, a uniform-random
// chooser picks `finish_arranging` about as often as any "add" move at every
// step and essentially never accumulates 3 cards in one slot — see the
// header's stress-point note. This is a concession to the RNG making
// progress, not a change to what counts as a valid meld.
function must_continue(p: Player) =
  (size_a(p) > 0 and size_a(p) < 4 and (sum over hand[p] as c: if extends_a(c, p) then 1 else 0) > 0) or
  (size_b(p) > 0 and size_b(p) < 4 and (sum over hand[p] as c: if extends_b(c, p) then 1 else 0) > 0) or
  (size_c(p) > 0 and size_c(p) < 4 and (sum over hand[p] as c: if extends_c(c, p) then 1 else 0) > 0)

move_type finish_arranging {
  when: not must_continue(actor)
  effect { arranging_done := true }
}

// === Card-point and meld-validity queries ===
//
// GAP: none of this reads a "declared minimal partition" — there is no such
// thing to read. Every function here either (a) reduces a per-card value
// from the game's own `ranking:` declaration, or (b) validates the *already
// assembled* contents of one small, fixed-size zone via a flat aggregate
// (`max == min`, `max - min == count - 1`). Finding the partition that
// minimizes deadwood over all ways to group a hand is exactly what none of
// this can express (see the file header and the report).

function suit_index(c: Card) = if c.suit == spades then 0 elif c.suit == hearts then 1 elif c.suit == diamonds then 2 else 3

// A=1, 2..10 face value, J/Q/K=10 — derived from `rank_value()` (see header).
function deadwood_points(c: Card) = if rank_value(c) + 1 > 10 then 10 else rank_value(c) + 1

function size_a(p: Player) = count over stageA[p] as c: true
function size_b(p: Player) = count over stageB[p] as c: true
function size_c(p: Player) = count over stageC[p] as c: true

function same_rank_a(p: Player) = (max over stageA[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == (min over stageA[p] as c: rank_value(c))
function same_rank_b(p: Player) = (max over stageB[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == (min over stageB[p] as c: rank_value(c))
function same_rank_c(p: Player) = (max over stageC[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == (min over stageC[p] as c: rank_value(c))

function same_suit_a(p: Player) = (max over stageA[p] as c: suit_index(c)) == (min over stageA[p] as c: suit_index(c))
function same_suit_b(p: Player) = (max over stageB[p] as c: suit_index(c)) == (min over stageB[p] as c: suit_index(c))
function same_suit_c(p: Player) = (max over stageC[p] as c: suit_index(c)) == (min over stageC[p] as c: suit_index(c))

// A slot is a valid meld iff it holds 3 or 4 cards and they are either all
// the same rank (a set) or all the same suit and consecutive (a run) — the
// max/min aggregate reduction the file header describes.
function meld_valid_a(p: Player) =
  (size_a(p) == 3 or size_a(p) == 4) and
  ( same_rank_a(p) or
    ( same_suit_a(p) and (max over stageA[p] as c: rank_value(c)) - (min over stageA[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == size_a(p) - 1 ) )
function meld_valid_b(p: Player) =
  (size_b(p) == 3 or size_b(p) == 4) and
  ( same_rank_b(p) or
    ( same_suit_b(p) and (max over stageB[p] as c: rank_value(c)) - (min over stageB[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == size_b(p) - 1 ) )
function meld_valid_c(p: Player) =
  (size_c(p) == 3 or size_c(p) == 4) and
  ( same_rank_c(p) or
    ( same_suit_c(p) and (max over stageC[p] as c: rank_value(c)) - (min over stageC[p] as c: rank_value(c)) == size_c(p) - 1 ) )

// Would adding card `c` to player `p`'s slot still be extendable toward a
// valid meld? Empty slots accept anything; otherwise the card must match
// the slot's established rank (toward a set) or match its suit and sit
// exactly one above its current max or one below its current min (toward a
// run growing at either end).
function extends_a(c: Card, p: Player) =
  size_a(p) < 4 and
  ( size_a(p) == 0
    or (same_rank_a(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageA[p] as x: rank_value(x)))
    or (same_suit_a(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageA[p] as x: rank_value(x)) + 1)
    or (same_suit_a(p) and rank_value(c) == (min over stageA[p] as x: rank_value(x)) - 1) )
function extends_b(c: Card, p: Player) =
  size_b(p) < 4 and
  ( size_b(p) == 0
    or (same_rank_b(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageB[p] as x: rank_value(x)))
    or (same_suit_b(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageB[p] as x: rank_value(x)) + 1)
    or (same_suit_b(p) and rank_value(c) == (min over stageB[p] as x: rank_value(x)) - 1) )
function extends_c(c: Card, p: Player) =
  size_c(p) < 4 and
  ( size_c(p) == 0
    or (same_rank_c(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageC[p] as x: rank_value(x)))
    or (same_suit_c(p) and rank_value(c) == (max over stageC[p] as x: rank_value(x)) + 1)
    or (same_suit_c(p) and rank_value(c) == (min over stageC[p] as x: rank_value(x)) - 1) )

// A player's total deadwood: whatever's still in hand, plus each slot's
// contents whenever that slot ISN'T a valid meld (an incomplete or
// abandoned grouping counts against the player exactly as if its cards had
// never left the hand).
function meld_deadwood(p: Player) =
  (sum over hand[p] as c: deadwood_points(c)) +
  (if meld_valid_a(p) then 0 else sum over stageA[p] as c: deadwood_points(c)) +
  (if meld_valid_b(p) then 0 else sum over stageB[p] as c: deadwood_points(c)) +
  (if meld_valid_c(p) then 0 else sum over stageC[p] as c: deadwood_points(c))
